          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>The student with 3+ blood and 2+ protein on dipstick, the child with periorbital</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>The student with 3+ blood and 2+ protein on dipstick, the child with periorbital puffiness, and the adult with cola-coloured urine after a sore throat all share pathology originating at which structure?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Renal pelvis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The renal pelvis collects urine from the calyces; its pathology causes obstructive symptoms and hydronephrosis, not the combination of haematuria and proteinuria seen in these patients.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Proximal convoluted tubule</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>PCT damage causes reabsorption defects such as glucosuria and aminoaciduria, not the combined haematuria and proteinuria described in these presentations.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Glomerulus</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>All three presentations arise from glomerular pathology. The glomerulus is the kidney's primary filtering unit, and damage to its filtration barrier produces both haematuria and proteinuria in varying degrees.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Collecting duct</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The collecting duct regulates water reabsorption under ADH control. Its dysfunction causes concentrating defects, not the pattern of haematuria and proteinuria seen here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "All three patients share the glomerulus as the common site of pathology — it is the kidney's primary filtering unit, and its damage produces both haematuria and proteinuria."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The glomerulus is the kidney's primary filtering unit. Damage at this site explains the hallmark combination of haematuria and proteinuria seen across diverse clinical presentations."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
