        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 185.425,
            "to": 195.425
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient presents with periorbital oedema and oliguria. Urine microscopy reveal</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with periorbital oedema and oliguria. Urine microscopy reveals fatty casts and oval fat bodies. Which clinical syndrome does this sediment indicate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Nephritic syndrome</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Nephritic syndrome is characterised by red blood cell casts, reflecting glomerular inflammation and haematuria — not fatty casts.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Nephrotic syndrome</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Fatty casts and oval fat bodies are pathognomonic for nephrotic syndrome. Massive proteinuria triggers hepatic lipoprotein overproduction, producing lipiduria that forms these characteristic casts.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Acute interstitial nephritis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Acute interstitial nephritis produces white blood cell casts, reflecting inflammation of the renal interstitium — not the lipid-laden casts seen here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Chronic kidney disease</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CKD is associated with broad, waxy casts formed in dilated, atrophic tubules — not the fatty casts and oval fat bodies described here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the cast–syndrome framework: fatty casts and oval fat bodies are pathognomonic for nephrotic syndrome, resulting from the lipiduria that accompanies massive proteinuria."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Matching cast type to syndrome is the sharpest microscopic clue in renal diagnostics: RBC casts = glomerulonephritis, WBC casts = pyelonephritis or AIN, fatty casts = nephrotic syndrome, granular and waxy casts = AKI or CKD."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
