          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 22-year-old man develops gross haematuria simultaneously with an upper respira</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 22-year-old man develops gross haematuria simultaneously with an upper respiratory tract infection. Serum complement levels are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>PSGN characteristically presents 1–3 weeks AFTER pharyngitis, not simultaneously, and serum C3 is typically low during the acute phase.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Goodpasture syndrome</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Goodpasture syndrome features linear IgG on immunofluorescence, pulmonary haemorrhage, and rapidly progressive GN — not isolated haematuria triggered by an upper respiratory infection.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>IgA nephropathy</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>IgA nephropathy is the world's commonest primary GN. Its hallmark is synpharyngitic haematuria — gross haematuria occurring simultaneously with the triggering infection, with normal complement levels.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>MPGN presents with nephrotic–nephritic overlap and characteristically low complement levels (low C3 alone in complement-mediated type, low C3 and C4 in Ig-mediated type) — complement is normal in this patient.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: simultaneous haematuria with infection + normal complement = IgA nephropathy. A latent gap of 1–3 weeks after pharyngitis + low C3 = PSGN. This timing–complement pair is the fastest diagnostic distinction."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The timing contrast is key: IgA nephropathy = synpharyngitic haematuria (simultaneous with infection) + normal complement; PSGN = latent haematuria (1–3 weeks after pharyngitis) + low C3."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
