          "label": "<p>How do ACE inhibitors slow the progression of chronic kidney disease?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>How do ACE inhibitors slow the progression of chronic kidney disease?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>They increase glomerular filtration rate to compensate for nephron loss</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>ACE inhibitors actually reduce GFR slightly in the short term. Their long-term benefit comes from reducing the damaging intraglomerular hypertension, not from increasing filtration.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>They relieve the intraglomerular hypertension that drives glomerulosclerosis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>By blocking angiotensin II, ACE inhibitors lower intraglomerular pressure, interrupting the self-amplifying cycle: nephron loss → compensatory hyperfiltration → glomerulosclerosis → further nephron loss.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>They stimulate erythropoietin production by the failing kidney</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>ACE inhibitors do not stimulate erythropoietin. The normocytic anaemia of CKD results from reduced erythropoietin production and is managed with exogenous EPO supplementation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>They directly reverse tubular atrophy in the renal cortex</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Tubular atrophy in CKD is an irreversible structural change. ACE inhibitors slow further damage by reducing intraglomerular pressure, but they do not reverse existing atrophy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the self-amplifying cycle: nephron loss → compensatory hyperfiltration → angiotensin II–driven intraglomerular hypertension → glomerulosclerosis → further nephron loss. ACE inhibitors break this cycle by reducing intraglomerular pressure."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "ACE inhibitors interrupt the self-amplifying cycle by blocking angiotensin II and relieving intraglomerular hypertension — the key haemodynamic force driving progressive glomerulosclerosis and nephron loss."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
